{/*
  The same caveats on every generated page. Written once so a page cannot state
  a different version of how the figures were arrived at, and so the disclosure
  the interactive chart makes in its hygiene note is made here too.
*/}
<section class="method">
  <h2>Where these numbers come from</h2>
  <p>
    Download counts are npm's own, published through its downloads API. They
    count installs rather than people, so they include CI, mirrors and repeated
    installs, and a package pulled in as a dependency counts for that package
    too.
  </p>
  <p>
    npm's API returns at most 549 days for a single package and 365 when several
    are compared, and truncates an over-long range rather than refusing it.
    npmpulse asks for the whole span in 365-day chunks and stitches the answers
    together, which is what makes a chart that starts in 2015 possible at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    npm reports 0 for every package from {DATA_START} until 2015-02-27, when its
    real records begin; those leading zeros are trimmed rather than charted.
    Individual days it lost entirely are interpolated, and the most recent day or
    two, which it has not finalised, is dropped. The monthly figures here are
    complete calendar months, so a package that started mid-month begins with the
    following one.
  </p>
  <p>
    A handful of months are inflated in npm's own figures. svelte's 2022-11-23
    records 5.0M downloads against a norm nearer 80K, and November 2022 is a spike
    in several packages at once; they are left alone here, because they are what
    the source says, but a one-month jump is treated as a spike rather than as a
    change of fortune. That is why the comparisons below only call a lead change
    a lead change when it held for a second month.
  </p>
  <p>
    A package's record can also begin before the package did: npm names get
    reused. Where a first day is a handful of downloads, that is what you are
    looking at — the earlier life of the name rather than a slow launch. Where it
    is hundreds of thousands, the opposite is true, and what is missing is
    upstream history rather than usage.
  </p>
  <p>
    Snapshot of {snapshot.generatedAt}. Figures run through {snapshot.lastDay}.
  </p>
</section>
